      "text": "The given triangles ABC and ABD share side AB. Knowing this, triangles ABC and ABD are similar because all of their corresponding angles are congruent (eg. angle A is adjacent to both A and A, angle B is adjacent to both B and B, and angle C is adjacent to both C and C.) By AA Similarity, triangles ABC and ABD are similar.\nWith triangles ABC and ABD being similar, their corresponding sides will be in proportion. Since all the sides of triangle ABC are longer than those of triangle ABD,\nthe ratio of the lengths of corresponding sides in triangle ABC =\nthe ratio of the lengths of corresponding sides in triangle ABD. Since\nthe ratio of those lengths is a rational number, we can also say that the ratio of those lengths is a whole number.\nAll three legs of triangle ABC are greater than the length of the hypotenuse (according to the diagram). Thus, we may multiply each leg of triangle ABD by 1.0, which merely extends the lengths of its sides by 1 unit.\nThe legs of triangle ABD that correspond to leg BD in triangle ABC are longer than the leg of triangle ABD that corresponds to AB. Thus, the length of BD will be enlarged more than the lengths of the corresponding legs of triangle ABC will be enlarged. In other words, the corresponding legs of ABD will be either less than, or more than, the corresponding legs of ABC. Thus the length of AB is 35 units. To find the length of BC, which is a leg of triangle ABC, we apply the Pythagorean Theorem \\[ BC = \\sqrt{AB^2 - AD^2} = \\sqrt{16^2 - 37^2} = \\boxed{35}. \\]. The answer is 35",
